    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "These solutions have two troublesome properties. First, unlike our simple example at the start of this chapter where there was a closed form estimator for the fixed effect $ \\mu $, the ML vector of fixed effects $ \\widehat{\\beta} $ is a function of the variance-covariance matrix $ \\widehat{V} $, which in turn contains the variance components that we wish to estimate. Second, because these solutions involve the inverse of $ \\widehat{V} $, they are nonlinear functions of the variance components. As a consequence, there is no simple one-step solution. ML estimation of $ \\beta $, $ \\sigma_{A}^{2} $, and $ \\sigma_{E}^{2} $ requires an iterative procedure, several steps of which are described below.",
